_candidates": ["Ectopic pregnancy: low-tier probability due to combined oral contraceptive use", "Ovarian torsion: possible but less likely than hemorrhage in this clinical context", "Acute cholecystitis: requires confirmation of Murphy's sign or fever >38.3°C", "Alcoholic gastritis: inconsistent with hypotension and RUQ localization"], "alarm_features": ["Hypotension with tachycardia (shock)", "Severe localized abdominal pain", "Recent significant weight loss"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecological Hemorrhage", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.15, "posterior": 0.18796596936516852, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Negative β-hCG testing combined with OCP use weakly reduces the urgency of gynecological hemorrhage as a life-threatening diagnosis, but hemodynamic instabilit…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Acute Biliary Tract Disease", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.25, "posterior": 0.44902981570568035, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["The absence of classic signs like Murphy's sign or fever in this hypotensive patient weakly reduces the likelihood of acute biliary tract disease, though RUQ p…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Hepatic Vascular Occlusion", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1, "posterior": 0.22451490785284017, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Chronic alcohol use combined with rapid weight loss moderately supports hepatic vascular occlusion (e.g., Budd-Chiari syndrome) due to known associations with …"], "evidence_against": ["The lack of neurological signs weakly disfavors hepatic vascular occlusion, but hypotension alone is insufficient to rule out this diagnosis in a patient with …"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["peliosis hepatis", "budd-chiari syndrome", "sinusoidal obstruction syndrome"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Pancreatitis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.004, "danger": 0.65, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of traditional pancreatitis markers (fatty stools, chronic history) combined with stable vital signs (except hypotension) strongly disfavors acute …"]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Traumatic Abdominal Injury", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.08, "posterior": 0.07184477051290886, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.55, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B6": {"id": "B6", "label": "Liver Vascular Abnormality", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.07, "posterior": 0.06286417419879527, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.45, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": ["peliosis hepatis", "budd-chiari syndrome", "sinusoidal obstruction syndrome"]}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3", "B5", "B6"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of Murphy's sign or fever (despite hypotension) contradict acute biliary tract disease as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "The absence of classic signs like Murphy's sign or fever in this hypotensive patient weakly reduces the likelihood of acute biliary tract disease, though RUQ pain persists."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the patient's daily alcohol consumption and recent rapid weight loss (from fitness prep) create conditions for Budd-Chiari syndrome through hepatic vein thrombosis?", "summary": "Chronic alcohol use combined with rapid weight loss moderately supports hepatic vascular occlusion (e.g., Budd-Chiari syndrome) due to known associations with thrombosis and metabolic stress."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's daily alcohol consumption directly support acute pancreatitis despite the absence of fatty stools or chronic pancreatitis history?", "summary": "Daily alcohol use alone provides weak support for acute pancreatitis, but the absence of chronic history or steatorrhea diminishes this association."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of oral contraceptive use and β-hCG testing align with the high danger threshold (0.9) of gynecological hemorrhage, requiring confirmation of whether pregnancy-related causes are…", "summary": "Negative β-hCG testing combined with OCP use weakly reduces the urgency of gynecological hemorrhage as a life-threatening diagnosis, but hemodynamic instability still warrants evaluation."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the absence of neurological signs (abducens palsy, auditory evoked potential abnormalities) in the vignette sufficient to challenge hepatic vascular occlusion despite hypotension?", "summary": "The lack of neurological signs weakly disfavors hepatic vascular occlusion, but hypotension alone is insufficient to rule out this diagnosis in a patient with risk factors."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of described fatty stool or chronic pancreatitis history undermine the likelihood of acute pancreatitis despite alcohol use?", "summary": "The absence of traditional pancreatitis markers (fatty stools, chronic history) combined with stable vital signs (except hypotension) strongly disfavors acute pancreatitis as the primary diagnosis."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.449], ["B3", 0.225], ["B1", 0.188]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Patient presents with hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm), consistent with shock or major haemodynamic instability. This requires immediate intervention.", "required_actions": ["Continue aggressive IV fluid resuscitation", "Monitor for response to fluids", "Prepare for potential blood transfusion or surgical intervention if internal bleeding is suspected"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (B2: Acute Biliary Tract Disease) has posterior 0.449 < 0.75, and dangerous alternatives (B1: 0.188 posterior, 0.9 danger; B3: 0.224 posterior, 0.85 danger) remain unresolved. No information exhaustion: askable discriminators (e.g., imaging, liver function tests, ultrasound for biliary stones or hepatic venous outflow) are still untried."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.9, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.44902981570568035, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Acute pancreatitis', 'Chronic pancreatitis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.449}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Acute Biliary Tract Disease", "posterior": 0.449, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "The absence of classic signs like Murphy's sign or fever in this hypotensive patient weakly reduces the likelihood of a…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B1", "B5"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=80%, source=both]\n\nAcute Biliary Tract Disease vs Hepatic Vascular Occlusion:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nAcute Biliary Tract Disease vs Traumatic Abdominal Injury:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: agitation, anxiety, bad taste in the mouth, blurred vision, bruising\n\nAcute Biliary Tract Disease vs Liver Vascular Abnormality:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: capillary fragility, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma, prolonged whole-blood clotting time\n\nHepatic Vascular Occlusion vs Traumatic Abdominal Injury:\n  Favours first:  abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n  Favours second: agitation, anxiety, bad taste in the mouth, blurred vision, bruising\n\nHepatic Vascular Occlusion vs Liver Vascular Abnormality:\n  Favours first:  abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n  Favours second: bruising susceptibility, capillary fragility, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma\n\nTraumatic Abdominal Injury vs Liver Vascular Abnormality:\n  Favours first:  agitation, anxiety, bad taste in the mouth, blurred vision, bruising\n  Favours second: bruising susceptibility, capillary fragility, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma\n\nNOT typically seen in Liver Vascular Abnormality: petechiae\n\nRelated to Acute Biliary Tract Disease: bile duct disease, bile reflux, biliary tract neoplasm (disease)\n\nRelated to Hepatic Vascular Occlusion: budd-chiari syndrome, hepatic infarction, hepatic vein thrombosis\n\nRelated to Liver Vascular Abnormality: hemophilia\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Acute Biliary Tract Disease\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Acute Biliary Tract Disease\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → pain → Hepatic Vascular Occlusion"}
